          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In formal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            a
            %em sequent
            (
            %a{:href => "#Gentzen"}> Gentzen 35
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Martin-L&#246;f"} Martin-Löf 83
            %em hypothetical judgement
            (
            %a{:href => "Pfenning-Davies"}> Pfenning, Davies 00
            ) is an expression in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metalanguage"} metalanguage
            which is a string of symbols of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Antecedent
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi Succedent
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Antecedent \vdash Succedent
          %p where
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Antecedent
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Antecedent
                are symbols indicating
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgements"} judgements
                called the
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antecedents"} antecedents
                or
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Succedent
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Succedent
                are symbols indicating
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgements"} judgements
                called the
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/succedents"} succedents
                or (if it is just a single judgement) the
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/consequent"} consequent
            %li
              %p
                the
                %strong consequence sign
                or
                = succeed "-symbol" do
                  %strong turnstile
                = succeed "\u201D" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊢
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Succedent
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Succedent
                is a
                %em consequence
                of
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Antecedent
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Antecedent
              %p
                “
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Antecedent
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Antecedent
                yields
                = succeed ".\u201D" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Succedent
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Succedent
              %p or
              %p
                “With
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Antecedent
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Antecedent
                the
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Succedent
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Succedent
                can be
                = succeed ".\u201D" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof"} proven
              %p or
              %p
                “
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Antecedent
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Antecedent
                , con-sequent-ly
                = succeed ".\u201D" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Succedent
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Succedent
              %p Or similar.
          %p
            Historically the “consequence” here was early on transmuted to “sequenz” (
            %a{:href => "#Gentzen"}> Gentzen
            ) and then later to “sequent”. See the section
            %em
              %a{:href => "#History"} History
            below.
          %p
            In systems of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+logic"} formal logic
            such as
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            such sequents express rules for explicit
            %em symbol manipulation
            admitted in the system rather than formal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/implications"} implications
            %em within
            the system. The latter instead are expressed by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+type"} function type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo :
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ψ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t : \phi \to \psi
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term+introduction+rule"} term introduction rule
            for terms of function types say that given one, one is allowed to get the other.
          %p Typically one allows a list of expressions on both sides of the turnstile-symbols as in
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Antec
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Antec
                    %mi k
                  %mo ⊢
                  %msub
                    %mi Succ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Succ
                    %mi l
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Antec_1, \cdots, Antec_k \vdash Succ_1, \cdot, Succ_l
          %p often abbreviated as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi Antec
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mover
                    %mi Succ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\vec Antec \vdash \vec Succ
          %p
            in which case on the left a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjunction"} conjunction
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antecedents"} antecedents
            and on the right a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjunction"} disjunction
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/succedents"} succedents
            is understood, as in
          %p
            “If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Antec
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Antec_1
            and
            = succeed "\u2026" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Antec
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Antec_2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Succ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Succ
                    %mi l
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Succ_1, \cdots, Succ_l
            is a consequence”.
          %p
            An
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic
              sequent
            has at most one succedent, which is then called the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/consequent"} consequent
            %em exactly
            one succedent, although technically it would make more sense to call those
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/minimal+logic"} minimal
            sequents.
          %p
            Another variant is that in some frameworks the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antecedent"} antecedent
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/succedent"} succedent
            displayed are required to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+variables"} free variables
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
            are instead displayed beneath the turnstile as in
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi true
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mover
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi true
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\vec \phi(x)\, true \vdash_{\vec x} \vec \psi(x) \, true
                  \\,.
          %p
            If the framework regards
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+types"} propositions as types
            then this is the same as writing
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo .
                  %mover
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mover
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\vec x. \vec \phi(x) \vdash \vec \psi(x)
                  \\,.
          %p Finally one can of course consider sequences of sequents
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Γ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊢
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Γ
                    %mi n
                  %mo ⊢
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\Gamma_1 \vdash \Delta_1), \cdots, (\Gamma_n \vdash \Delta_n)
          %p
            and if these are read
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjunction"} disjunctively
            it is like a higher-order sequent without
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antecedent"} antecedent
            and called a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypersequent"} hypersequent
          %p
            Rules for formal manipulation of sequents are called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent+calculi"} sequent calculi
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deduction+calculi"} deduction calculi
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            The precise nature of sequents has been formalized differently in different systems of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+logic"} formal logic
          %h3#intuitionistic_sequents Intuitionistic sequents
          %p
            (…) (
            %a{:href => "#Martin-L&#246;f"}> Martin-Löf
            ) (…)
          %h3#gentzens_sequents Gentzen’s sequents
          %p
            (…) (
            %a{:href => "#Gentzen"}> Gentzen
            ) (…)
          %h3#sequents_in_focalized_calculi Sequents in focalized calculi
          %p
            (…)
            %a{:href => "#Simmons"} Simmons
            (…)
          %h2#semantics Semantics
          %p
            We discuss aspects of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            of sequents, hence their interpretation when the ambient formal logic is regarded as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
          %h3#in_homotopy_type_theory In homotopy type theory
          %p
            Under the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            sequents in the type theory pretty accurately correspond to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+types"} dependent types
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            be an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Type
                  %mo ∈
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Type \in \mathbf{H}
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe+in+a+topos"} internal universe
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+objects"} small objects
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object+classifier"} object classifier
          %p
            This is defined as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representing object
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/core"} core
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+object"} small
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain+fibration"} codomain fibration
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} equivalence of ∞-groupoids
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi name
                  %mo :
                  %mi Core
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi X
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi small
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ≃
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Type
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  name
                  \:
                  Core(\mathbf{H}_{/X})^{small} \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} \mathbf{H}(X,Type)
                  \\,.
          %p
            This equivalence sends an
            = succeed "-family" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E \to X) \in \mathbf{H}_{/X}
            to its “name”, denoted
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi E
                  %mi Type
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X \stackrel{\vdash E}{\to} Type
                  \\,,
          %p
            which is the morphism characterized by fitting into an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-pullback"} ∞-pullback
            square of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi E
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mi Type
                            %mo ^
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇙
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mo ⊢
                              %mi E
                        %mtd
                          %mi Type
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  E &amp;\to&amp; \widehat{Type}
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;\swArrow&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  X &amp;\underset{\vdash E}{\to}&amp; Type
                  }
                  \\,,
          %p
            If here we simply replace, notationally, the arrow “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
            ” by the turnstile “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash
            ”, display a generic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+element"} generalized element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and then write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E(x)
            to highlight the dependence of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibers"} fibers
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mi E
                    %mi Type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \stackrel{\vdash E}{\to} Type
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi Type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x : X \vdash E(x) : Type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
            of which the morphism is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
          %p
            Similarly, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi t
                    %mi X
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \stackrel{t}{\to}_X E
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+element"} generalized element
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} slice (∞,1)-topos
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}_{/X}
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x : X \vdash t(x) : E(x)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"} term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependent type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
          %p
            In summary we have under the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+category+theory+and+type+theory"} relation between category theory and type theory
            the notational correspondence:
          %p
            %strong morphisms to sequents.
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos+theory"} ∞-topos theory
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi X
                        %mover
                          %mo →
                          %mrow
                            %mo ⊢
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi E
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Type
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;\;\;\;X \stackrel{\vdash \;\;\;\;E}{\to} \;\;\Type
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi X
                        %mover
                          %mo →
                          %mrow
                            %mo ⊢
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi t
                        %msub
                          %mrow
                          %mi X
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;\;\;\;X \stackrel{\vdash \;\;\;t}{\to} {}_X \;\;E
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo :
                        %mi X
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo :
                        %mi Type
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x : X \vdash E(x) : Type
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo :
                        %mi X
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo :
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x : X \vdash t(x) : E(x)
          %h2#History History
          %p
            The notion of sequent was introduced in section 2.3 of (
            %a{:href => "#Gentzen"}> Gentzen 1935
            ) (called
            %em Sequenz
            there). In (
            %a{:href => "#Martin-L&#246;f"}> Martin-Löf 1984, pages 29-30
            ) it says
          %blockquote
            %p
              The forms of hypothetical judgement
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              have
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              the form
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                    %mi true
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                    %mi true
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi A
                    %mi prop
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_1 true, \cdots, A_n true \vdash A prop
            %p
              which says that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is a proposition under the assumptions that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_1, \cdots, A_n
              are all true, and, on the other hand, the form
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                    %mi true
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                    %mi true
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi A
                    %mi true
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_1 true, \cdots, A_n true \vdash A true
            %p
              which says that the proposition A is true under the assumptions that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_1, \cdots, A_n
              are all true. Here I am using the vertical bar for the relation of logical consequence, that is, for what Gentzen expressed by means of the arrow
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
              in his sequence calculus, and for which the double arrow
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⇒
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow
              is also a common notation. It is the relation of logical consequence, which must be carefully distinguished from implication. What stands to the left of the consequence sign, we call the hypotheses, in which case what follows the consequence sign is called the thesis, or we call the
              %strong judgements that precede the consequence sign
              the antecedents and the
              %strong judgement that follows after the consequence sign
              the consequent. This is the terminology which Gentzen took over from the scholastics, except that, for some reason, he changed consequent into succedent and consequence into sequence, Ger. Sequenz, usually improperly rendered by sequent in English.
