          %h2#references References
          %p In Section 2.3 of
          %ul#Gentzen
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerhard+Gentzen"}> Gerhard Gentzen
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Untersuchungen über das logische Schließen I
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01201353"} doi
          %p
            what today is called a
            %em sequent
            is introduced under
            %em Sequenz
            (Ger:
            = succeed ")," do
              %em sequence
            %em Konsequenz
            (Ger:
            = succeed ")" do
              %em consequence
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
          %p In the lectures
          %ul
            %li{:id => "Martin-L&#246;f83"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Per+Martin-L%C3%B6f"}> Per Martin-Löf
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the Meanings of the Logical Constants and the Justifications of the Logical Laws
              %strong 1
              1 (1996) 11-60 &amp;lbrack;
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/MartinLofOnTheMeaning96.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            where the author provides a modern foundation for logic based on a clear separation of the notions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgment"} judgment
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
            (see at
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+dependent+type+theory"} Martin-Löf dependent type theory
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gentzen"} Gentzen
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antecedent"} antecedent
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/succedent"} succedent
            that Gentzen used.
          %p
            Referring explicitly to these lectures, these are are then just called
            %em hypothetical judgements
            in section 3 of
          %ul#Pfenning-Davies
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frank+Pfenning"}> Frank Pfenning
              , Rowan Davies,
              %em A judgemental reconstruction of modal logic
              (2000) (
              %a{:href => "http://www.cs.cmu.edu/~fp/papers/mscs00.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p In section D1.1 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sketches+of+an+Elephant"} Sketches of an Elephant
          %p
            sequents are introduced in the context of a basic introduction to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+logic"} formal logic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mover
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \vdash_{\vec c} \psi
            is used where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            is required to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec x
            are typeset below the turnstile. From the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            in section D1.2 it is clear that in the sense of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            these variables are to stand to the left of the turnstile.
